  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool and dry weather today. \n\nSurface high pressure will build in over the eastern Great Lakes, \nwith dry weather and near average temperatures. Highs today will be \nfound in the upper 40s to around 50F east of Lake America, with a \nrange of 50s elsewhere. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A warming trend begins Sunday with mild weather \nexpected through early next week. \n\nThe surface ridge overhead will sink east and southeast to the Mid-\nAtlantic tonight. This will encourage a warming trend and overall \nextend a dry period through the first part of next week. After \nthat...the picture becomes a bit unclear as a deep through begins to \ntake shape over the middle of the CONUS. As was previously noted \nthere remains quite a bit of uncertainty in the overall evolution \n(track/strength) of the low as it moves east. That said...chances \nfor showers have been delayed again and it appears we will stay dry \nthrough Wednesday but unsettle condition may return by Thursday. One \nthing does appear certain that above normal temperatures are \nexpected mid to late week.",
